A **method** is a named block of statements that does one job. Dividing a program into methods is **modular programming**: code is written once and reused, each part is tested separately, and a change is made in one place.

```java
    public static double milkBill(int litres, double rate) {
        return litres * rate;
    }
```

The **header** has the access specifier (`public`), a modifier (`static`), the return type (`double`), the name (`milkBill`) and the parameter list. The **prototype** is the return type, name and parameter list: `double milkBill(int litres, double rate)`. The **signature** is only the name and the parameter types in order: `milkBill(int, double)`. The return type and the parameter names are not part of it.

A **void** method returns nothing and is called as a statement. A method with another return type returns a value with **return**, and its call is used where the value is needed.

```java
    static String band(int age) {
        if (age < 13)
            return "Child";
        if (age < 20)
            return "Teen";
        return "Adult";
    }
    public static void main(String[] args) {
```

For age 15 the value is `Teen`. **return** ends the method at once, and a method may have several returns in different branches.

**Compiler checks:** a statement after return gives "unreachable statement"; a value-returning method with a path that has no return gives "missing return statement"; a return with a value in a void method gives "incompatible types: unexpected return value"; storing a double result in an int gives "incompatible types: possible lossy conversion from double to int".

**Formal parameters** are the variables in the header. **Actual parameters** are the values or variables in the call. When the method is called, the actual values are copied into the formal parameters in the same position, so the number, types and order must fit; a wrong number of arguments gives "method fare in class FareCalc cannot be applied to given types;".

```java
public class FareCalc {
    static int fare(int km, int rate) {
        return km * rate;
    }
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int distance = 7, perKm = 12;
        int total = fare(distance, perKm);
        System.out.println(total);
        System.out.println(fare(3, 15));
    }
}
```

The two calls print `84 / 45`. Here km and rate are formal parameters; distance, perKm, 3 and 15 are actual parameters.

**Order of a call:** the call is reached, the values are copied, the body runs, return sends the result back, the caller continues.

A primitive argument is passed **by value**: the formal parameter is a copy, so a change inside the method is lost.

```java
public class SwapTest {
    static void swap(int a, int b) {
        int t = a;
        a = b;
        b = t;
        System.out.println("In swap: " + a + " " + b);
    }
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int x = 10, y = 20;
        swap(x, y);
        System.out.println("In main: " + x + " " + y);
    }
}
```

Output: `In swap: 20 10 / In main: 10 20`. The values are interchanged inside swap only. To send a changed value back, return it: `Outside: 8500` is printed when raise returns pay + 500 and main stores it.

**Pass by reference** (definition and example only): the reference to the data is passed, so the formal and actual parameters refer to the same data and a change is seen by the caller. Example: a method that adds 200 to the data member weight of a Parcel object passed to it makes main print `1700`.

A **static** method belongs to the class; it is called by name in its own class or as `ClassName.method()`, and needs no object. A **non-static** method belongs to the objects; it can use their data members and is called through an object: `t.total(3)`.

```java
public class Tuition {
    int fee = 900;
    static String centre() {
        return "Sunrise Tuition";
    }
    int total(int months) {
        return fee * months;
    }
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println(centre());
        Tuition t = new Tuition();
        System.out.println(t.total(3));
    }
}
```

Output: `Sunrise Tuition / 2700`.

Because main is static, calling a non-static method by name gives "non-static method total(int) cannot be referenced from a static context", and a non-static data member used inside a static method gives "non-static variable fee cannot be referenced from a static context". Each object has its own data members: two TeaStall objects that sell different numbers of cups end with `17 20` cups.

A **pure method** returns a result without changing the state of the object (its data members) or its arguments; the same arguments give the same result. An **impure method** changes the state of the object or of an object passed to it.

```java
public class Rates {
    int stock = 50;
    int square(int n) {
        return n * n;
    }
    int issue(int n) {
        stock = stock - n;
        return stock;
    }
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Rates r = new Rates();
        System.out.println(r.square(6) + " " + r.square(6));
        System.out.println(r.issue(10) + " " + r.issue(10));
    }
}
```

Output: `36 36 / 40 30`. Two calls of square(6) give the same value; the two calls of issue(10) give different values because stock changes.

To classify a method, ask whether it changes any data member or passed object. A method that only displays text changes no data member, but printing is a side effect, so many teachers call such a method impure too; questions on purity normally avoid that borderline case and use methods that either compute from their arguments or change a data member.

**Method overloading** is defining two or more methods with the same name in a class but different parameter lists (number, type or order). It is **compile-time (static) polymorphism**: the compiler chooses the version from the arguments.

```java
    static int volume(int side) {
        return side * side * side;
    }
    static int volume(int l, int b, int h) {
        return l * b * h;
    }
    static double volume(double r, double h) {
        return 3.14 * r * r * h;
    }
    public static void main(String[] args) {
```

The calls print `64 / 30 / 628.0`.

A different return type or parameter name alone is **not** valid: "method area(int) is already defined in class Plots". With no exact match, arguments are widened (byte, short, int, long, float, double; char to int) and the nearest version is used: show('A') prints `int version: 65`. Two equally good versions give a compile-time error: "reference to mix is ambiguous".

